        "A local variable's name is available from its declaration to the end of its block. Separate calls to a function have their own parameters and local objects. The function void bump(int value) { ++value; } changes only a copy of the int; the original variable stays unchanged. To change a result without references, return the new value and assign it explicitly: count = bumped(count);.\n\nThe declaration int bumped(int value); tells the compiler the name, return type and parameters. You can place the definition with its body after main. The prototype must match the definition; it does not contain an implementation by itself. Parameter names in a prototype are optional: int bumped(int); is also valid when a name explains nothing — the compiler cares about the types and their order. A local object's lifetime usually ends when execution leaves the block. Do not use its name outside its scope.",
